sentences.t:
Entraron en Medina, sirvíalos Minaya,
todos fueron alegres del çerviçio que tomara[n],
el portero del rey quitar lo mandava;

sentences.l:
They-entered in Medina, served-them Minaya,
all were glad of-the service that they-had-received[n],
the porter of-the king to-settle it was-ordering;

sentences.t:
ondrado es mio Çid en Valençia do estava
de tan grand conducho commo en Medinal sacara[n];
el rey lo pagó todo, e quito se va Minaya.

sentences.l:
honored is my Çid in Valençia where he-was
by so great provision as in Medina-for-him they-had-brought-out[n];
the king it paid all, and quit himself goes Minaya.

sentences.t:
El obispo don Jero[me] buen cristiano sin falla,
las noches e los días las dueñas aguarda[va];
e buen cavallo en diestro que va ante su[e]s armas.

sentences.l:
The bishop don Jero[me] good Christian without fail,
the nights and the days the ladies was-attending[va];
and good horse on right-hand which goes before hi[e]s arms.

sentences.t:
Entrados son a Molina, buena e rica casa;
el moro Ave[n]galvón bien los sirvié sin falla,
de quanto que quisieron non ovieron falla,

sentences.l:
Entered are to Molina, good and rich house;
the Moor Ave[n]galvón well them was-serving without fail,
of as-much that they-wanted not they-had lack,

sentences.t:
Otro día manaña luego cavalgavan,
fata en Valençia sirvíalos sin falla;
lo so despendié el moro, que de[l]los non tomava nada.

sentences.l:
Other day morning at-once they-were-riding,
as-far-as in Valençia he-was-serving-them without fail;
the his-own was-spending the Moor, for of-them not he-was-taking nothing.

sentences.t:
Doz[ien]tos cavalleros mandó exir privado,
que reçiban a Minaya e a las dueñas fijas dalgo;
él sedíe en Valençia curiando e guardando,
ca bien sabe que Álbar Fáñez trahe todo recabdo;

sentences.l:
Two-[hun]dred knights he-ordered to-go-out quickly,
that they-receive to Minaya and to the ladies daughters of-something;
he was-sitting in Valençia keeping-watch and guarding,
for well he-knows that Álbar Fáñez brings all precaution;

and they-should-bring-him to Bavieca; little it-had-been that-him he-had-won
from-that king of Seville and of the his rout,
still not knew my Çid, he who in good hour girded sword,
if he-would-be runner or if he-would-have good stop;

sentences.t:
Reçebidas las dueñas a una grant ondrança,
obispo don Jero[me] adelant se entrava,
y dexava el cavallo, pora la capiella adeliñava;

sentences.l:
Received the ladies with a great honoring,
bishop don Jero[me] ahead himself was-entering,
and he-was-leaving the horse, toward the chapel he-was-making-his-way;

sentences.t:
En cabo del cosso mio Çid desca[va]lgava,
adeliñó a su mugier e a su[e]s fijas amas;
quando lo vio doña Ximena, a pie[de]s se le echava:

sentences.l:
At end of-the course my Çid was-dismounting,
he-made-straight to his wife and to hi[e]s daughters both;
when him saw lady Ximena, at feet herself to-him she-was-throwing:
